  <h2>Sound natural: signal the turn, then foreground the message</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Opening markers often form a short thought group followed by a brief boundary. The marker can be prominent when the logical turn itself is corrective, but the main content usually carries the final nucleus. Integrated parentheticals are often lighter. Stress is a discourse choice, not a permanent property of the written word.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Spoken line</th><th>Useful U.S. cue</th><th>Discourse effect</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>AdMITtedly, / the trial was SHORT.</td><td>/ədˈmɪtɪdli/ with a boundary after the frame</td><td>grant the limitation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Never-the-LESS, / it supports a LONGer test.</td><td>/ˌnevərðəˈles/ has primary stress on <span class="ex">less</span></td><td>mark the counterexpectation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>That SAID, / we need a CONtrol group.</td><td>weak <span class="ex">that</span> may approach /ðət/ before stressed <span class="ex">said</span></td><td>qualify the previous direction</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The trial, however, revealed a USEful pattern.</td><td>medial <span class="ex">however</span> can be a lighter parenthetical</td><td>integrate the turn without resetting fully</td></tr>
    <tr><td>On BALance, / a longer TRIal is defensible.</td><td>the final content nucleus carries the conclusion</td><td>close after weighing both sides</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>Do not announce every relation with maximum stress. Compare a strong corrective <span class="ex">HOWEVER, / that is not what the record says</span> with lighter integrated <span class="ex">The survey, however, was self-selected</span>. In spontaneous speech, a simple <span class="ex">but</span> or <span class="ex">still</span> may sound more natural than a chain of formal markers.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read granted limitation, final-stress nevertheless, weak-that counterpoint, light medial however, and balanced-conclusion listening check" data-success-message="Correct. You recovered granted limitation, counterexpectation, qualification, integrated contrast, and weighed conclusion from five U.S. English thought groups.">
    <h3>Listen for the logical turn and prominence</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Discourse listening choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a granted limitation">a granted limitation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a counterexpectation after the limitation">a counterexpectation after the limitation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a qualification before a new concern">a qualification before a new concern</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a light integrated contrast">a light integrated contrast</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a weighed final conclusion">a weighed final conclusion</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="five unrelated facts">five unrelated facts</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“AdMITtedly, / the trial was SHORT.”</span> The opening thought group signals <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a granted limitation" data-hint="The speaker accepts the short duration rather than disputing it." data-fix="Admittedly signals a granted limitation.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“Never-the-LESS, / it supports a LONGer test.”</span> Primary marker stress falls on <span class="ex">less</span> and signals <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a counterexpectation after the limitation" data-hint="The recommendation survives the obstacle." data-fix="Nevertheless signals a counterexpectation after the limitation.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“That SAID, / we need a CONtrol group.”</span> The speaker introduces <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a qualification before a new concern" data-hint="The new concern limits the direction of the preceding point." data-fix="That said introduces a qualification before the control-group concern.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Tutor reads the medial marker lightly: <span class="ex">“The trial, however, revealed a USEful pattern.”</span> The phrasing creates <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a light integrated contrast" data-hint="The parenthetical turn stays inside one continuing clause." data-fix="The lighter medial however creates an integrated contrast.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“On BALance, / a longer TRIal is defensible.”</span> The opening frame leads to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a weighed final conclusion" data-hint="The speaker closes after considering support and limitations." data-fix="On balance frames a weighed final conclusion.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    <details><summary>Tutor script and transcript</summary><ol><li>Admittedly, the trial was short.</li><li>Nevertheless, it supports a longer test.</li><li>That said, we need a control group.</li><li>The trial, however, revealed a useful pattern.</li><li>On balance, a longer trial is defensible.</li></ol><p>Read once in short thought groups and once inside a continuous argument. Keep the relation audible, but let the important limitation, recommendation, concern, pattern, or conclusion carry the final nucleus.</p></details>
  </div>

  <h2>Read a sustained argument without accepting its signposts blindly</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Fictional Westbridge evidence file</h3>
    <p>Westbridge opened the old library annex as an evening work and study hub from January 8 through March 1. It operated Monday through Thursday from 6:00 to 10:00 p.m. The fire-code capacity was 80. Entry logs recorded an average of 46 visits per evening. Registration records show 120 adults registered at least once, but the logs did not track unique repeat visitors.</p>
    <p>Sixty-eight users chose to answer an optional survey. Fifty-one reported having a quieter place to work, and 39 reported using the internet connection. Because the survey was voluntary, these percentages cannot represent every user or every resident. The trial occurred only in winter, had no comparison site, and did not measure employment, income, course completion, or exam results.</p>
    <p>The eight-week operating cost was $28,400. A time-limited grant paid $20,000, and the city paid $8,400. The grant is not confirmed for another year. On March 18, the committee considered a twelve-month extension at the same annex, with anonymized entry counts, an accessibility audit, and optional outcome surveys. It did not consider a permanent program or expansion to other sites.</p>
    <p>A defensible argument can concede the short, narrow trial; add the attendance and survey evidence; infer limits on causal and citywide claims; and conclude that a measured extension is worth considering. It cannot use <span class="ex">therefore</span> to turn attendance into employment impact or use <span class="ex">in other words</span> to replace the actual proposal with permanent expansion.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You reconstructed Westbridge's duration, hours, attendance, survey, evidence limits, funding, proposal scope, and defensible argument chain."><h3>Eight evidence-bound reading decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
